  "hypothesis_held": null,
  "conclusion": "False alarms: 0 of 6 undrifted months flagged, highest PSI 0.03130 against a 0.25 threshold, mean undrifted out-of-sample PR-AUC 0.5064. The detector is not simply flagging everything, which is what makes the delays below mean anything. covariate_shift_recency_x1.8: PSI flagged after 0 scoring month(s), max PSI 0.31099, PR-AUC 0.5538 -> 0.7079, 10% drop visible after None month(s) of matured labels, triggers ['RT-2', 'RT-3', 'RT-5', 'RT-6']. covariate_shift_one_segment: PSI flagged after 0 scoring month(s), max PSI 1.08399, PR-AUC 0.5538 -> 0.7405, 10% drop visible after None month(s) of matured labels, triggers ['RT-2', 'RT-3', 'RT-5', 'RT-6']. concept_drift_one_segment: PSI flagged after None scoring month(s), max PSI 0.03204, PR-AUC 0.5538 -> 0.3281, 10% drop visible after 0 month(s) of matured labels, triggers ['RT-1', 'RT-5', 'RT-6']. The two kinds of drift behave differently, and separating them is the result. Covariate shift is caught by PSI on the first scoring run, with no labels at all - and it does not necessarily hurt: widening a predictive feature raised PR-AUC rather than lowering it, so treating a PSI flag as evidence of damage would be wrong. Concept drift is the opposite: the feature distributions move far less, so PSI is quieter, and the harm shows only once labels mature. That is the PRD's expected observation, and it is why RT-1 and RT-4 (matured PR-AUC) exist alongside RT-2 and RT-3 (distributions): neither family of trigger would catch both cases. End to end on the platform: the scheduler's evaluation fired ['RT-2'] and queued run-2026-10-11-022, which read True the data that had arrived; the challenger reached selection: False (None), against champion v1. Parts: drift_flagged=True, retraining_started=True, challenger_compared=None, promotion_waited=True.",
  "limitations": [
    "In the end-to-end arm the retraining cooldown is 0 days for the scheduler's evaluation, because the champion is minutes old; the question is whether the triggers fire, and publish's own evaluation with the configured 7-day cooldown is recorded beside it. Its promotions are decided by the harness acting as the approvers, so they measure that promotion waits for a decision, not how long a person takes to make one.",
    "Replay is over synthetic months, not Online Retail II. Only the generator can be told to shift one feature or change one segment's behaviour on demand, which is what the independent variable requires.",
    "The end-to-end arm did not test every part: the scheduler's evaluation fired ['RT-2'] and queued run-2026-10-11-022, which read True the data that had arrived; the challenger reached selection: False (None), against champion v1. Parts: drift_flagged=True, retraining_started=True, challenger_compared=None, promotion_waited=True."
  ],
